'\" t
.\" blas-microbenchmark(1). make turns this .in into blas-microbenchmark.1,
.\" then into the site's blas-microbenchmark.html; edit the .in.
.\" man/test_man_*.sh check it against the programs: see
.\" doc/dev/documentation.md.
.TH BLAS-MICROBENCHMARK 1 2026-10-16 "blas-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation, no justification: both hurt in a terminal, and
.\" hyphenation splits literals such as "# label:".
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
blas-microbenchmark, bmb_dasum, bmb_daxpy, bmb_dcopy, bmb_ddot, bmb_dnrm2,
bmb_dscal, bmb_dswap, bmb_saxpy, bmb_sdot, bmb_zdotc, bmb_dgemv, bmb_dger,
bmb_dsymv, bmb_dsyr, bmb_dsyr2, bmb_dtrmv, bmb_dtrsv, bmb_sgemv, bmb_dgemm,
bmb_dsymm, bmb_dsyrk, bmb_dsyr2k, bmb_dtrmm, bmb_dtrsm, bmb_sgemm, bmb_cgemm,
bmb_zgemm, bmb_strsm, bmb_ztrsm, bmb_ssyrk, bmb_zherk,
bmb_zhemm \- microbenchmarks for BLAS routines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmb_\c
.I routine
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each
.BI bmb_ routine
times one BLAS routine, called through CBLAS, over a sweep
of sizes and thread counts, and prints one row per point: the time of a
call and the rate it achieved.
All of them take the same options.
They run against the BLAS library chosen when the package was built
(OpenBLAS, BLIS, AMD AOCL-BLAS, Netlib reference BLAS, NVPL, ArmPL or
Intel MKL), or, built
against FlexiBLAS, the one FlexiBLAS loads when they start
.RB ( FLEXIBLAS ,
see
.BR ENVIRONMENT );
.B \-\-version
says which.
.PP
To compare libraries, build the package once against each and run the
same command with each build, or build it once against FlexiBLAS and run
the command under each library; then turn the results into charts with
.BR bmb_report (1).
.SS Routines
Level 1 routines work on vectors, level 2 on a matrix and a vector,
level 3 on matrices:
.PP
.TS
l l.
Level 1	dasum daxpy dcopy ddot dnrm2 dscal dswap
\&	saxpy sdot zdotc
Level 2	dgemv dger dsymv dsyr dsyr2 dtrmv dtrsv
\&	sgemv
Level 3	dgemm dsymm dsyrk dsyr2k dtrmm dtrsm
\&	sgemm cgemm zgemm strsm ztrsm ssyrk zherk zhemm
.TE
.PP
The first letter is the precision, as in BLAS:
.B s
single,
.B d
double,
.B c
single complex,
.B z
double complex.
Every operation is measured in double precision, and the most used ones
in the others too: single precision moves half the bytes and fits twice
as many numbers in a vector register, and a complex multiply\-add does
four times the arithmetic of a real one on twice the data.
Three exist for complex numbers only: zdotc, the dot product with the
first vector conjugated, and zherk and zhemm, the Hermitian counterparts
of dsyrk and dsymm.
.PP
Level 1 routines are sized with
.BR \-v .
Level 2 and 3 routines are sized with
.B \-m
and, for those with two dimensions,
.BR \-M .
Which dimension is which:
.PP
.TS
l l.
dgemv sgemv dger dsymm zhemm	\-m is M, \-M is N
dtrmm dtrsm strsm ztrsm	\-m is M, \-M is N
dgemm sgemm cgemm zgemm	\-m is M and K, \-M is N
dsyrk ssyrk zherk dsyr2k	\-m is N, \-M is K
dsymv dsyr dsyr2 dtrmv dtrsv	\-m is N (square; no \-M)
.TE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-x, \-\-warmup " n
Untimed calls before measuring each point.
Default: 1.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-iterations " n
Timed samples taken at each point, at least.
Default: 10.
.TP
.BI "\-w, \-\-warmup\-time " s
Calls repeated for
.I s
seconds before the first point of each thread count, on top of
.BR \-x :
the CPU leaves its idle clock, and the library wakes its threads.
0 turns it off.
Default: 0.2.
.TP
.BI "\-T, \-\-min\-time " s
Samples taken past
.B \-i
until they add up to
.I s
seconds.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI "\-e, \-\-max\-spread " percent
Then samples taken until their interquartile range is at most
.I percent
of their median, judged on 10 samples at least, or
.BR \-i .
Default: 0, off.
.TP
.BI "\-E, \-\-max\-time " s
Sampling stops once the samples add up to
.I s
seconds, whatever
.B \-T
and
.B \-e
ask; at most 100000 samples are taken.
Default: 10.
.TP
.BI "\-b, \-\-batch " n
Calls averaged per sample;
0 chooses automatically, per point (see
.BR "HOW A NUMBER IS MEASURED" ).
.B \-\-batch 1
times every call on its own.
dtrmv, dtrsv, dtrmm, dtrsm, strsm and ztrsm are always timed that way:
their operand is restored before every call, and a larger
.B \-b
is ignored with a warning.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI "\-v, \-\-vector\-size " sweep
Vector sizes, for level 1 routines.
Default: 10000000, past any last-level cache, so that the bandwidth is
the memory's.
.TP
.BI "\-m, \-\-matrix\-dim1 " sweep
First matrix dimension, for level 2 and 3 routines.
Default: 5000 at level 2, a matrix past any last-level cache, and 2000
at level 3, under a second a dgemm call on one core.
None is a power of two (see
.BR \-p ).
.TP
.BI "\-M, \-\-matrix\-dim2 " sweep
Second matrix dimension.
Without it, matrices are square: the second dimension follows the first at
every point.
With it, every combination of the two is measured.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-thread\-count " sweep
Number of BLAS threads.
Default: 1.
See
.BR THREADS .
.TP
.B "\-s, \-\-statistics"
Also report, per point, the mean of the samples, their standard deviation
(over the samples as measured, divided by
.I n
rather than
.IR n \-1)
and the slowest sample, as columns in that order after the rates; in
the table, the batch size too, which the CSV and JSON always have.
The median and the spread come after them, so that a table or a CSV
read by column position keeps the columns it had before 1.8.0.
.TP
.B "\-c, \-\-verify"
Before timing each point, check that the library computes the right
result there; stop with exit status 2 if it does not.
See
.BR "CHECKING RESULTS" .
Default: on.
.TP
.B "\-C, \-\-no\-verify"
Do not check the results.
.TP
.BI "\-L, \-\-layout " order
How the matrices are stored and passed to CBLAS:
.B row
(row-major, as C stores arrays) or
.B col
(column-major, as Fortran and LAPACK do).
A library may handle the two differently: CBLAS row-major calls are
often turned into column-major ones on the transposed problem, so a
Fortran program can see other numbers than the default does.
The problem is the same either way (the same sizes, the upper triangle,
no transpose); only its storage changes.
Ignored, with a warning, by the vector routines.
Default: row.
.TP
.BI "\-k, \-\-cache " state
Where the operands of each timed call are:
.B hot
reuses them from call to call, so that those that fit are in the caches,
as in a code calling the routine in a loop on the same data;
.B cold
rotates over copies of them, enough that the others reach past twice
the last-level cache, so that each call finds its own out of the caches,
as in a code that touches much other data in between.
See
.BR "HOW A NUMBER IS MEASURED" .
Default: hot.
.TP
.BI "\-a, \-\-align " bytes
Align every operand to
.I bytes
(a power of two from 8 to 2097152, a huge page), so that no library is
measured on operands it finds misaligned.
Default: 64, a cache line.
.TP
.BI "\-p, \-\-ld\-pad " n
Add
.I n
elements (up to 65536) to every matrix's leading dimension: the distance
in memory between two of its rows, or its columns with
.BR "\-L col" .
A size that is a power of two, such as 4096, puts every row
on the same cache sets, which slows some libraries at exactly that size;
a few elements of padding steps off it.
Ignored, with a warning, by the vector routines.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-label " text
Tag the results, for what no automatic field records: a BIOS setting, a
NUMA binding, SMT off.
1 to 200 characters, no control characters.
Default: none, and no label is written.
.TP
.BI "\-o, \-\-output " file
Also save the results to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI "\-f, \-\-output\-format " format
.B csv
or
.B json
for
.BR \-o .
Default: guessed from the extension of the
.B \-o
file, otherwise csv.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Show the version, the BLAS library and the machine, without running
anything.
.SS Sweeps
The four size options each take a
.IR sweep :
.PP
.TS
l l l.
\fImax\fP	that one size	4096
\fImin\fP:\fImax\fP	doubling	256:4096 \(-> 256 512 1024 2048 4096
\fImin\fP:\fImax\fP:\fIstep\fP	linear	1000:4000:1000 \(-> 1000 2000 3000 4000
\fIv1\fP,\fIv2\fP,...	exactly those	64,1000,4096
.TE
.PP
.I max
is always measured, even when the step would overshoot it:
100:1000:300 gives 100, 400, 700 and 1000.
A sweep has at most 256 points.
A size whose memory would not fit in
.B size_t
is refused before anything is allocated.
.SH HOW A NUMBER IS MEASURED
Reading the clock costs tens of nanoseconds, which is the whole duration of
a small level 1 call.
So each
.I sample
times a
.I batch
of calls and divides by the batch size.
The batch size is chosen per point, so that a batch lasts long enough for
the clock not to matter;
calls that already take longer get a batch of 1, so nothing changes for
level 3 or for large sizes.
.B calls_per_sample
gives the batch size chosen.
.PP
The reported time is the
.B fastest
sample, not the mean.
A sample is already an average over a whole batch, so the fastest is not a
lucky call: it is the batch that ran with the least interference.
One scheduling hiccup inflates a sample a great deal, and would drag a
mean with it.
How steady the samples were goes with it, in every output: their median,
and their quartiles, interpolated between the samples either side as R
and NumPy do; the table gives the interquartile range as a percentage of
the median,
.BR "IQR [%]" .
.B \-s
adds the mean, the standard deviation and the slowest sample.
.PP
Ten samples say little about noise, and a small point measured in a
millisecond may run before the CPU has left its idle clock.
.B \-w
warms up for a time rather than a number of calls, once a thread count.
.B \-T
takes as many samples as fill a time, and
.B \-e
as many as it takes for them to agree:
.RS
.EX
bmb_ddot \-v 1024:1048576 \-T 0.1 \-e 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
The spread is the interquartile range, not the coefficient of variation:
one preempted sample in thousands keeps a standard deviation from ever
settling, and a quartile does not see it.
The samples taken are in the results, and in the table when
.BR \-T " or " \-e
is given.
The fastest of more samples is faster still: compare runs taken with the
same settings, which the results record.
.PP
Two rates are derived from that time:
.TP
.B GFLOP/s
the conventional operation count of the routine (2\(muM\(muN\(muK for
dgemm, K\(muN\(mu(N+1) for dsyrk, ...) divided by the time.
Not shown for dcopy and dswap, which do no arithmetic.
Complex routines count real operations: a complex multiply\-add is four
real multiplications and four additions, so each counts four times its
real counterpart's operations (cgemm and zgemm 8\(muM\(muN\(muK where
dgemm counts 2\(muM\(muN\(muK; zherk four times dsyrk's), and their
GFLOP/s compare with the real routine's.
.TP
.B GB/s
the bytes the routine must move \(em each operand read once, each result
written once, counted the way STREAM counts them, 4 bytes an element in
single precision and 8 in double \(em divided by the time.
Levels 1 and 2 only: level 3 routines reuse their operands from cache, so
a rate built from that traffic would not measure bandwidth.
The comparison with STREAM holds for operands out of the caches: past the
last-level cache, or at any size with
.BR "\-k cold" ;
a smaller vector measured hot is read from a cache, at that cache's
bandwidth.
.PP
.B \-k cold
sets up as many copies of the operands as it takes for the others to
reach twice the last-level cache, from what the operands of one call
take, and gives each call the next one; a copy whose operand the routine
overwrites is restored right after its call, never before the next, which
would bring it back into the cache.
Once the operands alone reach that far, there is one copy, each call
pushing its own operands out.
The copies take about twice the last-level cache in memory, on top of
one set of operands.
The copies are at most 16384: below the size where that is not enough,
the points are partly warm, and a warning says from which size.
The size of the last-level cache comes from
.IR /sys ;
where it cannot be read,
.B \-k cold
is refused.
A cold call on a small vector waits for memory more than it streams from
it, so its GB/s are below STREAM's.
.SH CHECKING RESULTS
A benchmark times a call whatever it computes: a library that gets the
result wrong \(em a broken build, a bug at one size or thread count \(em
can look fast.
So each point is first checked, unless
.B \-C
is given: one call on the operands about to be timed,
at that size and thread count, compared with a reference computed
independently.
.PP
Level 1 and 2 results are recomputed directly, which costs about one
more call.
Level 3 results are checked by random projection: for a random vector
.IR r ,
.I C*r
is compared with
.IR A*(B*r) ,
and for dtrsm, strsm and ztrsm the residual
.I U*(X*r)
with
.IR B*r .
That takes as long as a level 2 call, where recomputing
.I C
would take as long as the call itself, and a wrong
.I C
gives a wrong
.I C*r
for almost every
.IR r .
Routines that update one triangle are also checked to leave the other
as it was.
.PP
The tolerance is a bound on rounding error, proportional to the number
of terms in each element and to their magnitudes: a correct library is
well inside it, a wrong one far outside.
In single precision the reference is still computed in double, and the
bound half a billion times wider: it catches a library that computes
the wrong thing, but not a small error in a long sum (at a million
elements, sdot is only checked to within the size of its terms).
A wrong result is reported with the routine, the size, the thread count
and the element that differs; nothing more is measured, and no
.B \-o
file is written.
The check needs memory for a few vectors the size of the result, and
for daxpy, saxpy, dswap, dgemv, sgemv and dsymv a copy of the vector
they write.
.PP
The check is on by default, as it is in STREAM and HPL: a number from a
library that computes the wrong thing is worth less than none.
.B \-C
turns it off, for a library already checked, or where that memory
matters.
.SH THREADS
.B \-t
sets the number of threads through the library's own interface.
If an environment variable that the library reads says otherwise,
.B \-t
wins, and a warning says so:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 bmb_dgemm \-t 1
OMP_NUM_THREADS is ignored! Set to 4 but option \-t is set to 1.
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without
.BR \-t ,
those variables apply as usual: the first one set gives the thread count.
A value that is not a whole number of threads, from 1 to 2147483647
(0, \-2, 2,1, or one with a blank in it), is ignored with a warning, and
the next variable is read.
Netlib reference BLAS is single-threaded, and
.B \-t
has no effect with it.
MKL may run a small problem on fewer threads than it is given
.RB ( MKL_DYNAMIC ,
on by default);
.B MKL_DYNAMIC=false
makes it use them all.
AOCL-BLAS does the same (AOCL Dynamic), unless the library was built
with
.BR \-\-disable\-aocl\-dynamic .
.PP
Nothing here sets thread affinity, and operands are allocated and filled by
the main thread, so on a machine with several NUMA nodes every page lands
on that thread's node.
Both matter as soon as more than one thread runs; see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
Threads beyond the CPUs the process may run on take turns, and the
timings measure that.
A warning says so when the largest thread count is more than those CPUs:
the ones its affinity allows
.RB ( taskset ,
a Slurm job, a cpuset), capped by the CPU quota of its cgroup (cgroup v2
.IR cpu.max ,
which
.B docker run \-\-cpus
sets).
.SH HARDWARE COUNTERS
GFLOP/s and GB/s say how fast, not why.
Hardware counters \(em instructions, cycles, cache misses, the
floating-point operations the CPU actually executed \(em say why, and the
benchmarks leave reading them to the tools made for it,
.BR perf (1)
and
.BR likwid\-perfctr ,
run around them.
Both count the whole process: allocating and filling the operands, the
.B \-\-verify
check, the batch calibration and the warm-up, not only the timed calls.
.PP
Two runs that differ only in
.B \-i
take that away.
With
.B \-b 1
each sample is one call, preceded by the operand restore of the routines
that need one, so the difference between the two counts, divided by the
difference in
.BR \-i ,
is what one call costs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
perf stat \-e instructions,cycles \-\- bmb_dgemm \-x 0 \-b 1 \-i 10 \-m 1024
perf stat \-e instructions,cycles \-\- bmb_dgemm \-x 0 \-b 1 \-i 110 \-m 1024
.EE
.RE
.PP
Here, one call is a hundredth of the difference.
Keep to one size and one thread count per pair of runs, and to sizes
where a call takes long enough that the clock read around it does not
count.
.PP
The floating-point events have CPU-specific names, which
.B perf list
gives: for instance
.B fp_arith_inst_retired.256b_packed_double
.RB ( _single
for the s and c routines)
on recent Intel CPUs, or
.B fp_ret_sse_avx_ops.all
on AMD Zen.
.B likwid\-perfctr
groups them per CPU, under names most CPUs share
.RB ( FLOPS_DP ,
.B FLOPS_SP
for the s and c routines,
.BR L3 ;
.B likwid\-perfctr \-a
lists the groups of the one at hand), and pins the threads to the cores
it measures:
.PP
.RS
.EX
likwid\-perfctr \-C 0\-3 \-g FLOPS_DP bmb_dgemm \-x 0 \-b 1 \-i 10 \-t 4 \-m 4096
.EE
.RE
.PP
Most virtual machines, containers and WSL expose no hardware counters,
and
.B perf
then reports them as not supported.
On a real machine, reading them may need
.I /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid
at 2 or lower for
.BR perf ,
or the access daemon or root for
.BR likwid\-perfctr .
.SH OUTPUT
Results go to standard output, one row per point, as they are measured;
warnings and errors go to standard error.
Every result starts with where it came from:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# blas\-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@
# backend: openblas (OpenBLAS 0.3.26 DYNAMIC_ARCH Haswell MAX_THREADS=64)
# verified: every point, against a reference, before it was timed
# layout: row\-major
# cpu: Intel(R) Core(TM) Ultra 7 155U (14 logical CPUs, 1 NUMA node)
# caches: L1d 48K, L1i 64K, L2 2M, L3 12M
# frequency: governor performance, turbo off
# os: Linux 6.6.87 x86_64
# date: 2026\-10\-02T09:05:04Z
# affinity: CPUs 0\-13
# command: bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-o dgemm.json
# run: 0.2 s and 1 call of warm\-up, 10 samples, batch size chosen per point, hot cache
# operands: aligned to 64 bytes, leading dimensions + 0
# compiler: gcc 13.3.0, CFLAGS \-g \-O2 \-fopenmp
# environment: OMP_PROC_BIND=close
# routine: dgemm
.EE
.RE
.PP
The library's version string, where it exposes one, tells apart two
builds of the same library.
The caches show where a sweep leaves each level.
The frequency line gives the CPU frequency governor (several, joined by
.BR / ,
when CPUs differ) and whether turbo is on, as Linux exposes them in
.IR /sys ;
virtual machines and containers often expose neither.
When either lets the frequency change during the run, a governor other
than
.B performance
or turbo on, a warning on standard error says so and gives the commands
that fix it; the run goes on.
A field that cannot be read on a machine is left out, never guessed.
The hostname is deliberately not recorded.
.PP
The next lines say how the run was made, so that it can be made again:
the CPUs the process could run on, as Linux lists its affinity; the
command line, the program by its name and each word as a shell would
take it back (as typed: a path given to
.B \-o
goes with the results, a relative one keeps a home directory out);
the warm\-up time and calls, the samples and the time and spread that
may ask for more, and the batch size
.RB ( \-w ", " \-x ", " \-i ", " \-T ", " \-e ", " \-E ", " \-b ,
which is 1 whatever
.B \-b
says for the routines whose operand is restored before every call),
and the cache
.RB ( \-k );
the operands' alignment and, for the routines with a matrix, the padding
of their leading dimensions
.RB ( \-a ", " \-p );
the compiler and the CFLAGS the benchmarks were built with (not the BLAS
library's own);
and the variables set among those that change what a BLAS library does:
.BR OMP_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR OMP_PROC_BIND ,
.BR OMP_PLACES ,
.BR OMP_DYNAMIC ,
.BR OMP_WAIT_POLICY ,
.BR GOMP_CPU_AFFINITY ,
.BR GOMP_SPINCOUNT ,
.BR KMP_AFFINITY ,
.BR KMP_BLOCKTIME ,
.BR KMP_HW_SUBSET ,
.BR OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR GOTO_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR OPENBLAS_CORETYPE ,
.BR BLIS_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR BLIS_JC_NT ,
.BR BLIS_PC_NT ,
.BR BLIS_IC_NT ,
.BR BLIS_JR_NT ,
.BR BLIS_IR_NT ,
.BR BLIS_ARCH_TYPE ,
.BR AOCL_ENABLE_INSTRUCTIONS ,
.BR MKL_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR MKL_DOMAIN_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR MKL_DYNAMIC ,
.BR MKL_THREADING_LAYER ,
.BR MKL_ENABLE_INSTRUCTIONS ,
.B MKL_CBWR
and
.BR FLEXIBLAS .
With
.BR \-l ,
a
.B "#\ label:"
line follows the backend, and unless
.B \-C
was given, a
.B "#\ verified:"
line comes next, then, for the routines with a matrix, the
.B "#\ layout:"
.RB ( \-L ).
.PP
With
.BR \-o ,
the results are also saved to a file:
.TP
.B csv
the same comment lines, then a header and one row per point.
Readers must skip lines starting with
.BR # ,
e.g.\&
.B pd.read_csv(file, comment=\(dq#\(dq)
in pandas.
.TP
.B json
one object holding the same information as fields:
.BR version ,
.BR backend ,
.BR blas ,
.BR label ,
.BR verified ,
.B layout
.RB ( row " or " col ,
for the routines with a matrix),
.BR date ;
.B machine
with
.BR cpu ,
.BR logical_cpus ,
.BR numa_nodes ,
.BR caches ,
.BR governor ,
.B turbo
(true or false)
and
.BR os ;
.B run
with
.B command
(the words of the command line),
.BR warmup ,
.BR warmup_time ,
.BR iterations ,
.BR min_time ,
.BR max_spread ,
.BR max_time ,
.B cache
.RB ( hot " or " cold ),
.BR align ,
.B ld_pad
(for the routines with a matrix),
.B batch
(when every point used the same:
.B \-b
or 1, as above),
.BR compiler ,
.BR cflags ,
.B cpus_allowed
and
.B environment
(the variables set, by name);
.B routine
and, naming its dimensions,
.B dim1_label
and
.BR dim2_label .
Then a
.B results
array, one object per point:
.BR thread_count ,
.BR dim1 ,
.BR dim2 ,
.BR time_s ,
.BR gflops ,
.BR gbytes_per_s ,
with
.B \-s
.BR mean_s ,
.B stddev_s
and
.BR max_s ,
then
.BR calls_per_sample ,
.BR median_s ,
.BR q1_s ,
.B q3_s
and
.BR samples ,
and with
.BR "\-k cold" ,
.BR copies ,
the copies of the operands the point rotated over, which the table and
the CSV give last too.
A field that does not apply is left out.
This is what
.BR bmb_report (1)
reads.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every point was measured and every result written;
1 for an invalid option or size, or when standard output or the
.B \-o
file could not be written \(em a table cut short by a full disk must not
pass for a complete one;
2 when a result was wrong (see
.BR "CHECKING RESULTS" ),
in which case nothing is written to the
.B \-o
file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The thread-count variables the library reads, in the order it reads them:
.TP
OpenBLAS
.BR OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR GOTO_NUM_THREADS ,
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
.TP
BLIS, AOCL-BLAS
.BR BLIS_NUM_THREADS ,
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
.TP
NVPL, ArmPL
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
.TP
MKL
.BR MKL_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR OMP_NUM_THREADS .
The count is set for MKL's BLAS domain, which overrides
.BR MKL_DOMAIN_NUM_THREADS ;
a warning says so when it is set.
.B MKL_DYNAMIC=false
makes MKL use them all on a small problem too (THREADS).
.TP
FlexiBLAS
those of the library it loaded, as above; none for Netlib.
.PP
.B FLEXIBLAS
picks the library a build against FlexiBLAS loads, by the name
.B flexiblas list
gives it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
FLEXIBLAS=OPENBLAS\-OPENMP bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-o openblas.json
FLEXIBLAS=BLIS\-OPENMP bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-o blis.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
The results name the library loaded: backend
.BR flexiblas/openblas\-openmp ,
and FlexiBLAS's version with the library's name.
.PP
.BR OMP_PROC_BIND " and " OMP_PLACES
pin the threads of the OpenMP-threaded libraries.
.PP
The results record these variables, and the others that change what the
library does, when they are set (OUTPUT).
.PP
.BR NO_COLOR ,
when set and not empty, turns off the colours of warnings and errors,
which are only used on a terminal anyway.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @pkglibexecdir@/level1
.TQ
.I @pkglibexecdir@/level2
.TQ
.I @pkglibexecdir@/level3
The benchmarks, grouped by BLAS level rather than put on the
.BR PATH .
To run them by name:
.RS
.PP
.EX
BMB=@pkglibexecdir@
export PATH=\(dq$BMB/level1:$BMB/level2:$BMB/level3:$PATH\(dq
.EE
.RE
.TP
.I @pkgdatadir@/reframe
ReFrame tests of the benchmarks, for a site's test suite: one per
routine, its result checked
.RB ( \-\-verify ),
its GFLOP/s and GB/s as performance variables, the site's references
read from a JSON file.
The README there says how to set the sizes, the threads and the
references; see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.SH EXAMPLES
Sweep ddot from 1K to 16M elements, with the statistics, saved as CSV:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_ddot \-v 1024:16777216 \-s \-o ddot.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same with the vectors out of the caches at every size, for the
memory's bandwidth rather than a cache's:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_ddot \-v 1024:16777216 \-k cold \-o ddot\-cold.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every point is checked before it is timed. For a library already
checked, skip that:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 512:4096 \-t 1:8 \-C \-o dgemm.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
The sizes that matter to you, and nothing else:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 1024,2048,4096
.EE
.RE
.PP
Square matrices, then every shape (the grid
.BR bmb_report (1)
draws as a heatmap):
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048                 # 512\(S2, 1024\(S2, 2048\(S2
bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-M 512:2048     # all 9 combinations
.EE
.RE
.PP
Thread scaling, threads pinned \(em OMP_* for the OpenMP-threaded
libraries, taskset for a pthread-built OpenBLAS:
.PP
.RS
.EX
OMP_PROC_BIND=close OMP_PLACES=cores bmb_dgemm \-t 1:8 \-o dgemm.json
taskset \-c 0\-7 bmb_dgemm \-t 1:8 \-o dgemm.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
On a machine with several NUMA nodes, spread the pages over all of them,
or measure one node at a time \(em usually what you want to compare:
.PP
.RS
.EX
numactl \-\-interleave=all bmb_dgemm \-t 1:32
numactl \-\-cpunodebind=0 \-\-membind=0 bmb_dgemm \-t 1:16
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two conditions on the same machine, told apart in the report by their
labels (turbo is recorded, but a series is not split by it):
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 1024:8192 \-l \(dqturbo on\(dq  \-o turbo\-on.json
bmb_dgemm \-m 1024:8192 \-l \(dqturbo off\(dq \-o turbo\-off.json
bmb_report turbo\-*.json > report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
In a ReFrame test suite: every benchmark of this install, each one
verified, its rates reported as performance variables, and their results
made into a report:
.PP
.RS
.EX
R=@pkgdatadir@/reframe
reframe \-c $R/blas_microbenchmark.py \-n \(aqbmb_run\(aq \-r
bmb_report output/*/*/*/*/result.json > report.html
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bmb_report (1),
.BR numactl (8),
.BR taskset (1)
.PP
The guides \(em installing, comparing libraries, on an HPC cluster,
ReFrame \(em and this manual online:
https://mathieucaissa.github.io/blas\-microbenchmark/
.PP
The source:
https://github.com/MathieuCAISSA/blas\-microbenchmark
